Janis Papanagnou <janis_papanagnou@hotmail.com> wrote:
> Patric Mueller wrote:
>
>> Careful, the soldier in the watchtower near the river might be more
>> dangerous than you'd think.
>
> I don't think this part of the design is perfect; the problem is that
> there's no incentive to cross the river at that point. The unaware
> player who visits the level the first time might get the (intended?)
> frustrating experience,

Well, even an uninformed player at this stage of the game should be
able to survive at least one shoot of a wand of fire. If not he will
probably have more problems with the four random dragons on this
level.

But I'm not sure if I shouldn't remove the watchtower. The bottleneck
of the river crossing is dangerous enough even without the watchtower.

The watchtower was an early idea that IMO got better implemented in
the other level and the watchtower doesn't really fit into the
landscape of this level.

> and the experienced player would just avoid
> coming close to the tower and choose to cross the river south-east,
> thereby avoiding the straight line of fire.

One great surprise when testing this level was that crossing the river
at the bottom of the map was not necessarily less dangerous than
crossing it east of the watchtower.

There are 4 random dragons on this level and if at least one of them
is a red dragon than one shouldn't try to walk across the frozen parts
of the river. And of course the possibility that one of the soldier
has a wand of fire isn't slim either.

The first time I tried to cross the river east of the watchtower, I
evaded the direct line to the guard in the tower until I stood on the
first ice cell. The guard shoots with his wand, the ice melts, I drop
into the water and crawl to the ice east of my former position, the
ice there melts from the *same* fire ray and after dropping into the
river I have crossed the river.

Bye
